      <purpose>To record whether an individual has achieved a developmental milestone.</purpose>
      <keywords>development</keywords>
      <keywords>developmental</keywords>
      <keywords>milestone</keywords>
      <keywords>child</keywords>
      <keywords>infant</keywords>
      <keywords>skill</keywords>
      <keywords>attainment</keywords>
      <keywords>surveillance</keywords>
      <use>Use to record whether an individual has achieved a specific skill recognised as a developmental milestone, at the time of assessment. The skill may relate to any developmental domain, including movement, language, cognition, social and emotional development, or self-care.&#xD;
&#xD;
This archetype is designed to:&#xD;
- inform clinical assessment of an individual's development, including the need for further assessment or referral;&#xD;
- form part of a child health record;&#xD;
- support exchange of developmental information between healthcare providers and families; and&#xD;
- support clinical decision support, such as reminders to reassess outstanding milestones.&#xD;
&#xD;
Achievement may be recorded by a healthcare professional, or by a parent or carer, for example in a parent-held child health record. It may also be recorded retrospectively, where the skill was achieved at a different time to the date of assessment.&#xD;
&#xD;
Developmental milestones are typically recognised for infancy, childhood and adolescence. A skill recorded as 'Not achieved' is considered outstanding, with the expectation that, in typical development, it will eventually be achieved. Developmental delay may occur in some individuals, such as those with a physical or intellectual disability, in which case some milestones may not be achieved until adulthood. In clinical systems, an outstanding skill can continue to be presented to the clinician at later visits until its achievement is recorded, or until a clinician decides it is no longer relevant to reassess. Once a skill is achieved, the achievement remains part of the individual's developmental history, even if the skill is later temporarily or permanently lost.&#xD;
&#xD;
This archetype is intended to complement OBSERVATION.functional_ability. Functional ability records an individual's current ability to perform an activity at any age, including partial ability and change over time. This archetype records the first achievement of a developmental skill, or that it has not yet been achieved.</use>
      <misuse>Not to be used to record an individual's ability to perform an activity, including where they can only partly perform it. Use OBSERVATION.functional_ability for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record loss of a previously achieved skill. Use OBSERVATION.functional_ability for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record a clinical interpretation of whether a skill has been achieved at the expected age. Use a specific archetype for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record a diagnosis of developmental delay, developmental disorder, or another condition affecting the achievement of developmental skills. Use EVALUATION.problem_diagnosis for this purpose.&#xD;
&#xD;
Not to be used to record items or scores from a standardised developmental assessment instrument. Use specific archetypes for this purpose.</misuse>
